{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 38, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a powerful figure in the Crusader states during the 12th century. He ruled the County of Tripoli from 1146 until his death in 1193. His reign marked a period of significant expansion and consolidation of Tripoli's territory and influence in the Levant. Raymond III was a skilled military leader and a shrewd politician navigating complex relationships with both Christian and Muslim powers.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension*\n\nRaymond was the son of Raymond I Count of Tripoli. He inherited the county upon his father's death in 1146. His early reign was characterized by military successes against Muslim forces particularly in the region of Antioch. He solidified Tripoli's control over its coastal territories and expanded its influence into Syria. Raymond III was known for his piety and devotion to the Catholic faith which helped him maintain alliances with European powers. He actively participated in the Third Crusade alongside King Louis VII and Emperor Frederick Barbarossa. During the Crusade Raymond fought against Saladin's forces and played a key role in the siege of Acre.\n\n*Reign and Policies*\n\nRaymond III's rule was marked by a focus on strengthening Tripoli's economy and military. He invested in infrastructure building fortifications and improving the county's defenses. He also fostered trade and agriculture contributing to economic prosperity. His reign witnessed a significant population growth and an increase in the number of Christian settlers in Tripoli. Raymond III was a staunch defender of the Crusader states and actively worked to maintain their independence. He engaged in diplomatic efforts to secure alliances and negotiate treaties with neighboring rulers. He maintained a complex relationship with the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum balancing cooperation and competition.\n\n*Legacy and Decline*\n\nRaymond III’s reign i